Take notes about the video, and what phonological features are discussed.
 - the lot vowel (o in <job> or <politics>) -> american english tends to replace it with other vowel sounds, such as a
 - the bath vowel (a in <behalf>, <path> or <passed>) -> american english tends to use an æ sound.
 - the t sound, as americans tend to pronounce it like a d
 - the yod (approximant j in <new> or <duty>) -> american english tends to drop it, and while british english may pronounce <du> dʒ, american english pronounce it d.
 - the r sound, which in british english is pronounced less
 - the h drop (usually in american english, where <human> is pronounced /'u/<man>)

